WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.680
In this lesson, we're looking at tool distribution across agents,

2
00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:08.560
specifically why giving an agent fewer tools almost always produces better results.

3
00:00:09.279 --> 00:00:14.000
We'll cover the cost of tool bloat, how overlapping tools break routing,

4
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:20.079
and the specialist sub-agent pattern that keeps each agent's context clean and focused.

5
00:00:20.079 --> 00:00:20.879
Let's get into it.

6
00:00:22.079 --> 00:00:26.639
Ship an agent 30 tools and it pays for all 30 on every single turn,

7
00:00:26.639 --> 00:00:28.319
whether it calls them or not.

8
00:00:28.319 --> 00:00:29.680
That's tool bloat.

9
00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:34.639
The anti-pattern where an agent carries more tools than its job needs.

10
00:00:34.639 --> 00:00:39.599
Every tool description loads into context, so extra tools cost tokens,

11
00:00:39.599 --> 00:00:43.919
slow inference, and crowd out content the model actually needs.

12
00:00:43.919 --> 00:00:49.200
The fix is the specialist sub-agent, an agent configured with a narrow palette

13
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:54.720
matched exactly to its role, which makes behavior more predictable and easier to debug.

14
00:00:55.279 --> 00:00:59.119
Context isolation is the property that falls out of it.

15
00:00:59.119 --> 00:01:03.200
When each sub-agent only sees the tools relevant to its job,

16
00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:05.360
the orchestrator's window stays clean.

17
00:01:05.919 --> 00:01:09.360
Audit your list and remove anything the agent never calls.

18
00:01:10.639 --> 00:01:14.160
I want you to hold two mental models side by side.

19
00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.080
First, the bloated agent.

20
00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:20.239
It receives every tool in the catalog because it was easier to configure that way.

21
00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:24.559
Its context fills up with tool descriptions for things it will never call.

22
00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:29.199
When two tools overlap, the model picks between them inconsistently.

23
00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:33.599
Responses slow down and the agent becomes harder to trust in production.

24
00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:36.559
Now compare that to the specialist agent.

25
00:01:36.559 --> 00:01:39.440
It receives only the tools it needs for its mission.

26
00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:44.000
The model always sees one obvious candidate for each request.

27
00:01:44.639 --> 00:01:48.959
Routing is clean, behavior is consistent, and when something goes wrong,

28
00:01:48.959 --> 00:01:50.959
you know exactly which tool to look at.

29
00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:55.199
Specialization is an investment that pays back in reliability.

30
00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:00.239
Tool routing is just the model's selection of which tool to call,

31
00:02:00.239 --> 00:02:04.559
and it makes that call almost entirely from the tool descriptions you wrote.

32
00:02:04.559 --> 00:02:07.839
That's why overlap is such a subtle reliability risk.

33
00:02:07.839 --> 00:02:12.960
When two tools could plausibly handle the same request, the model doesn't flip a coin.

34
00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:15.279
It picks based on description quality,

35
00:02:15.279 --> 00:02:18.880
and the pick may change with small variations in the user's input.

36
00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:22.960
Users expect the same action to trigger the same tool every time,

37
00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:26.720
so alternating between two similar tools produces divergent outcomes

38
00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:29.600
that are hard to diagnose because the input looks identical.

39
00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:33.759
The fix is to make sure each tool owns a clearly distinct scope.

40
00:02:34.479 --> 00:02:39.679
Merge the overlapping pair or rewrite their descriptions until the distinction is unambiguous.

41
00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:44.160
Four principles for scoping tools to agents.

42
00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:46.720
First, one agent, one mission.

43
00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:49.279
Each agent should have a defined role,

44
00:02:49.279 --> 00:02:52.479
and the tools it receives should map directly to that role.

45
00:02:53.039 --> 00:02:54.880
Second, tools earn their slot.

46
00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:57.759
Don't add a tool speculatively.

47
00:02:57.759 --> 00:03:00.720
Add it when no existing tool covers the need,

48
00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:04.720
and consider removing it if usage data shows it's rarely called.

49
00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:07.600
Third, audit the catalog.

50
00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:12.639
Tools added during early prototyping often stick around long after they're useful.

51
00:03:12.639 --> 00:03:17.520
A quarterly review of tool assignments catches this drift before it becomes a problem.

52
00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:20.720
Fourth, name precision matters.

53
00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:24.479
If two tools have similar names or vague descriptions,

54
00:03:24.479 --> 00:03:26.479
the model can't distinguish them cleanly.

55
00:03:27.039 --> 00:03:28.960
Invest time in description quality.

56
00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:32.320
It's the highest leverage thing you can do for routing accuracy.

57
00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:38.320
The specialist sub-agent pattern is the most practical way to apply these principles at scale.

58
00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:40.639
The idea is straightforward.

59
00:03:40.639 --> 00:03:45.039
You decompose a complex agent workflow into distinct roles,

60
00:03:45.039 --> 00:03:49.520
and you configure each sub-agent with only the tools that role requires.

61
00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:53.119
A retrieval sub-agent gets search and fetch tools.

62
00:03:53.119 --> 00:03:55.199
It doesn't need file write access.

63
00:03:55.199 --> 00:03:58.399
A write sub-agent gets database and file tools.

64
00:03:58.399 --> 00:04:00.800
It doesn't need search capabilities.

65
00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:05.759
The orchestrator coordinates between them without touching domain-specific tools directly.

66
00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:11.360
Each sub-agent's tool palette is small enough that the model can always identify the right call.

67
00:04:11.919 --> 00:04:16.480
Because the roles are isolated, you can test each sub-agent independently.

68
00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:21.359
You can also swap out one sub-agent's implementation without touching the others.

69
00:04:21.359 --> 00:04:25.040
That's a significant operational advantage as your system grows.

70
00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:30.799
We've spent this lesson arguing for lean tool palettes, so let's address the exception.

71
00:04:31.359 --> 00:04:34.480
Some tools should appear on multiple agents, and that's fine.

72
00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:36.959
Logging is the obvious case.

73
00:04:36.959 --> 00:04:40.239
If you want consistent observability across your system,

74
00:04:40.239 --> 00:04:44.320
every agent that produces output should have a logging tool.

75
00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:46.079
Status reporting is similar.

76
00:04:46.079 --> 00:04:51.119
An orchestrator and its sub-agents may all need to write to a shared status store

77
00:04:51.119 --> 00:04:53.760
so you can track workflow progress from a single view.

78
00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:56.799
The key distinction is intent.

79
00:04:56.799 --> 00:05:02.959
Cross-cutting tools are deliberately shared because their scope is genuinely broader than one role.

80
00:05:02.959 --> 00:05:06.880
Tool bloat happens when tools end up on agents by accident

81
00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:09.519
because no one cleaned up after a prototype.

82
00:05:09.519 --> 00:05:12.160
Minimize by design, not by accident.

83
00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:18.399
Focused tool palettes reduce context overhead, sharpen routing,

84
00:05:18.399 --> 00:05:20.959
and make your agents easier to test and trust.

85
00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:28.000
The specialist sub-agent pattern puts that into practice by matching each agent's tools to its role,

86
00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:33.440
and cross-cutting tools like logging stay shared on purpose rather than by accident.

87
00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:38.880
Start lean, add deliberately, and audit the catalog as your system grows.

88
00:05:39.519 --> 00:05:40.880
That's it for this lesson.

89
00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:42.079
I'll see you in the next one.

